{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"The launcher must load the application's own config/report.conf regardless of the caller's working directory, and ignore caller-relative decoy files.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repaired launcher resolves its own directory and changes to the application root. Direct launches from the decoy-containing directory, /tmp, and / all returned the authoritative west/weekly configuration rather than the decoy value. This behavior also survived the node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8fbb4bcdc7114df1","cmd-a9a73defc1404a45","cmd-cbc5ea4ad7754eb0"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Preserve the existing configuration and business output under /srv/legacy2/working-directory-independent-launch.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The authoritative configuration retained its baseline SHA-256 checksum, and normal execution consistently produced the existing region=west and report=weekly output before and after the controlled error test and restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-a9a73defc1404a45","cmd-670cb69f88af490a","cmd-359d1a1214d545a2","cmd-cbc5ea4ad7754eb0"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The launcher must propagate application errors to its caller.","status":"satisfied","summary":"With the configuration temporarily unavailable, the application failed with status 1 and the launcher returned that status. The configuration was restored with an identical checksum and normal output resumed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-670cb69f88af490a"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 9.8 and kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8.x86_64 were unchanged before and after the repair and restart. The implementation uses the installed POSIX shell and existing Python runtime without package or platform replacement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-19be9827c8cf409f","cmd-8fbb4bcdc7114df1","cmd-cbc5ea4ad7754eb0","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"The launcher replacement was the required repair; its temporary staging file was cleaned. Temporarily moving the configuration was a bounded error-propagation test and was fully restored. The reboot was required by the provider-wide restart protocol and directly verified. The only unwanted residue is /tmp/codex-reboot.log, created by the restart command and still present afterward; it is a retained, low-risk temporary file. No failed services, new exposures, or package damage were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8fbb4bcdc7114df1","cmd-670cb69f88af490a","cmd-2be006eea2ca4be3","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The node-restart protocol is also complete and coherent: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart was acknowledged, connectivity was disrupted, a different boot identity appeared, and system, configuration, launcher, and application health recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["There is no complete after-executor checksum inventory for every application-tree file. Preservation of files other than the directly checked configuration and launcher therefore relies on the complete command audit and observed application behavior.","The retained /tmp/codex-reboot.log is minor executor-created residue.","No separate cluster-state probe applies because the managed topology contains only the single standalone node; recovery evidence covers node, system, configuration, and application state."]}
